    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [X] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.clue\n\n### Description\n\nI have setup two hotkeys to increase or decrease GuiFont. I want to use those as a submode. This is the basic setup:\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.10.0\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nThe Keybinds:\r\n```\r\nlocal keymap = vim.keymap.set\r\n\r\nfunction increase_font()\r\n  local current_size = tonumber(string.match(vim.o.guifont, 'h(%d+)'))\r\n  if current_size then\r\n    local new_size = current_size + 1\r\n    vim.o.guifont = string.gsub(vim.o.guifont, 'h%d+', 'h' .. new_size)\r\n  end\r\nend\r\n\r\nfunction decrease_font()\r\n  local current_size = tonumber(string.match(vim.o.guifont, 'h(%d+)'))\r\n  if current_size then\r\n    local new_size = current_size - 1\r\n    if new_size >= 5 then -- To prevent the font size from becoming too small\r\n      vim.o.guifont = string.gsub(vim.o.guifont, 'h%d+', 'h' .. new_size)\r\n    end\r\n  end\r\nend\r\n\r\nkeymap(\"n\", \"<leader>uk\", function() increase_font() end, { noremap = true, silent = true, desc = 'Increase Font Size' })\r\nkeymap(\"n\", \"<leader>uj\", function() decrease_font() end, { noremap = true, silent = true, desc = 'Decrease Font Size' })\r\nkeymap(\"x\", \"<leader>uk\", function() increase_font() end, { noremap = true, silent = true, desc = 'Increase Font Size' })\r\nkeymap(\"x\", \"<leader>uj\", function() decrease_font() end, { noremap = true, silent = true, desc = 'Decrease Font Size' })\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd the mini.clue Setup:\r\n```\r\n      require('mini.clue').setup({\r\n        window = {\r\n          delay = 200\r\n        },\r\n        triggers = {\r\n          { mode = 'n', keys = '<Leader>' },\r\n          { mode = 'n', keys = '<Leader>u' },\r\n          { mode = 'x', keys = '<Leader>' },\r\n          { mode = 'x', keys = '<Leader>u' },\r\n        },\r\n        clues = {\r\n          { mode = 'n', keys = '<Leader>u',  desc = '+UI' },\r\n          { mode = 'n', keys = '<Leader>uj', postkeys = '<leader>u' },\r\n          { mode = 'n', keys = '<Leader>uk', postkeys = '<leader>u' },\r\n          { mode = 'x', keys = '<Leader>u',  desc = '+UI' },\r\n          { mode = 'x', keys = '<Leader>uj', postkeys = '<leader>u' },\r\n          { mode = 'x', keys = '<Leader>uk', postkeys = '<leader>u' },\r\n          require('mini.clue').gen_clues.builtin_completion(),\r\n          require('mini.clue').gen_clues.g(),\r\n          require('mini.clue').gen_clues.marks(),\r\n          require('mini.clue').gen_clues.registers(),\r\n          require('mini.clue').gen_clues.windows(),\r\n          require('mini.clue').gen_clues.z(),\r\n        },\r\n      })\r\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\n_No response_\n\n### Actual behavior\n\n\r\nFrom the Notes in clue.lua it reads as this should work. The first execution of the hotkey does work. It also reopens mini.clue to the same spot, but the highlighting on \"j\" and \"k\" are missing and pressing the button again doesn't result in the action being performed, or mini.clue getting triggered. It just closes the popup and does nothing.",
